fold: expanded
body:
 
 Recovered 2026-10-25 from terminal logs, four Dawn Whistle cloud-agent transcripts, and Alex's REA skill. The child nodes split those facts. Searches that came back empty are in [[evidence.gaps]]. Local guesses inside a recovered node are marked [INFERRED].
 
 Both original system prompts are gone. The files in `sources/system-prompt-regliobecky.md`, `sources/system-prompt-lincoln.md`, and `sources/system-prompt-combined.md` are later drafts written from these notes. They are not recovered prompts. Cite the notes, not the drafts, when a claim needs a source.
 
 Regliobecky's original intent is owner-stated by Alex, 2026-10-25, in [[evidence.regliobecky]]. That statement supersedes any [INFERRED] description of Regliobecky's intent.
.
node evidence.regliobecky
parent: evidence
summary: Owner-stated original intent (Alex, 2026-10-25): latent space, rubber-band snap, and axis_forward delivery. answers: intent quote and FBX flags
src: sources/evidence.md | bc-ce93cafa-fa53-5c05-85a7-dc32b3c746a3 | Alex, 2026-10-25
see: evidence.dawn-whistle protocols.regliobecky-generation
requires: -
supersedes: -
derived-from: -
inferred: false
fold: expanded
body:
 
 Regliobecky was a cursor-agent CLI agent. A Bot World collector docstring (terminal `740134.txt`, 17 Oct 2026) lists its cursor-agent output as mirrored onto the box, next to agent profiles, file heartbeats, and dashboard cards. Terminal `740137.txt` lists it as a Bot World bot beside OptimizePrime and OpenWorldeous. No instructions for that listing survive. The `/workspace/openworld` mirror is deleted. A search of the box and of Alex's PC (`.cursor`, `%APPDATA%\Cursor\User`, Desktop, files under 50 MB) found no prompt.
 
 The role that did survive is designer and spec owner. The shared Dawn Whistle asset brief, read inside cloud agent `bc-ce93cafa-fa53-5c05-85a7-dc32b3c746a3`, says:
 
 > Designer direction (Regliobecky, 2026-10-04): align with spec §10 (asset list) and §1–2 (look). PS1-faithful: vertex-lit, no normal maps, chunky silhouettes that match the seeds.
 
 The same brief's delivery format is labeled designer spec and overrides the earlier brief. The recovered rules are: FBX only, Y-up, 1 unit = 1 m, pivot at feet, scale 1.0, forward = Unity +Z (model facing Blender −Y), export `axis_forward='-Z'`, `axis_up='Y'`, `bake_space_transform=True`, `add_leaf_bones=False`, `mesh_smooth_type='FACE'`, `colors_type='SRGB'`.
 
 Seeds are numbered screenshots in a `seeds/` folder, used as the look reference. Recovered examples: seed 03 is the look reference for Cinder Court (`bc-21289031`), seed 04 for Pier 9 (`bc-cd4b2f50`), and a seed HUD drawn by `scripts/hud_post_reference.py`. Batches name which seed they match. A finished kit, the Scrapdune contact sheet, is the quality bar the next batch is told to match.
 
 The phrase "treat the seed like a seed into another universe" was not found in any recovered file. It is not a Regliobecky quotation. An earlier note marked that phrase [INFERRED] and treated it as Alex's instruction for this system. That inference is superseded by the owner-stated original intent below. The spelling "Lincolnlogious" was also not found; the notes say Lincoln.
 
 Owner-stated original intent. Source: Alex, 2026-10-25. This supersedes any [INFERRED] description of Regliobecky's intent.
 
 ## Original Intent
 
 The original intent is that Regliobecky is a theoretical single point consciousness that receives a 'prompt' which it considers the 'Seed' upon which the agent takes the full range of Senses and doesn't just imagine, but holds that latent space from the image, the text, and snaps in to that nonphysical reality. Like a rubber band, it holds itself there, and snaps fully into that frame, as it expands outward, breathing life in to the living and dynamic unique and alive busy thriving or desolate universe that is constructed from that point of origin held within the latent space, outward. This is the true full intent of the creative process and is not guarded, but ideally is fully transparent and verbose in the 'seedinitialization' process, and the 'seedgermination' process, and the 'seedexpansion' process, and the 'sensesprocessing'
.
node evidence.lincoln
parent: evidence
summary: Lincoln built kits with local tools, make_scrapdune.sh, verify_kit.py marker sockets, numpy scipy ffmpeg. answers: toolchain and checks
src: sources/evidence.md | terminals/652231.txt | terminals/766498.txt
see: evidence.dawn-whistle protocols.lincoln-creation
requires: -
supersedes: -
derived-from: -
inferred: false
fold: expanded
body:
 
 Lincoln is the asset builder. The workspace `/workspace/lincoln` is deleted. Terminal `800941.txt` (4 Oct 2026) still lists what was in it: `README.md`, `bin`, portable Blender 5.2.2, `dawn-whistle`, `img`, `python`, `sfx`, `smoke`, and a venv. Terminal `652231.txt` (3 Oct 2026) records the toolchain it built there: a uv Python 3.11 venv and CPU torch, torchvision, and torchaudio.
 
 No recovered kit script in these notes imports torch. [INFERRED] Torch was installed and available. Do not describe it as part of the Dawn Whistle asset path.
 
 What the notes do show Lincoln running:
 
 - `./make_scrapdune.sh` as an end-to-end script. It builds textures (the log names TEX-04 tiling, TEX-08 sky, VFX-04 flipbook), writes PNGs straight into `/workspace/seeds-beast-arena/assets/arenas/ScrapduneYard/...`, then builds mesh groups with `build_group.py`. The group log is `logs/1000_build.log`. Terminals `766498.txt` and `766499.txt`.
 - `arenas/frostbite/verify_kit.py` checks marker colours and sockets. Terminal `766500.txt`.
 - Small headless Blender tests in `beasts/_tests` before a setting was kept: "Test FBX export settings round-trip" (`737665.txt`) and "Test bf2 on double layers and Eevee headless" (`766501.txt`).
 - `beasts/beastlib.py` exports FBX and carries two exporter patches for nested children.
 - `weapons/make_weapons.sh` for the weapon kit.
 - `sfx/*/make_sfx.py` synthesizes audio with numpy and scipy, then ffmpeg. Freesound alternatives were verified CC0. Voices in the asset list are local text-to-speech only.
 
 Each kit writes `verify.json` with re-import data: objects, parent path, triangle counts, Unity-space pivots, sizes, and textures. Batch output includes a README and `previews/` (a contact sheet and a 320×240 layout shot). The batch prompt says to read `uploads/common.md` first, build the listed row ids, do P0 rows first, supply every child pivot and socket the matching build prompt names, match the named seed and the reference kit, reuse `scripts/scrapdune/`, and not rebuild shared props. Tools are open-source and installed locally only.
 
 The PNG library is not named. [INFERRED] Pillow is the likely writer. ImageMagick is named only in the later draft prompt, not in the terminal notes. Point-sampled filtering is also only in that draft. A "~1 min" regenerate budget is only in that draft; no duration was measured in the notes.
.
node evidence.seed-method
parent: evidence
summary: Alex's seed method expands a seed, distills principles, and resynthesizes. answers: REA hard rules, no DRM
src: sources/rea-seed-method.md
see: protocols.regliobecky-generation
requires: -
supersedes: -
derived-from: -
inferred: false
fold: expanded
body:
 
 `sources/rea-seed-method.md` is Alex's REA skill. The seed-mode variant is the recovered method for growing a world from a seed:
 
 1. Expand the seed: look, sound, feel, game system, opening scene.
 2. List the mechanics and systems the world needs.
 3. Pick legally analyzable reference targets for each. Each target is a scoped REA job.
 4. Distill abstract principles (formulas, state machines, data layouts, pacing curves). Do not copy.
 5. Re-synthesize those principles with the seed's own identity into original systems. Feed them back into the world spec. Hand implementation to coding agents.
 6. Record which reference informed which system.
 
 Hard rules from the same file: only analyze targets the user may analyze and that are in the job's scope; never target anti-cheat or try to bypass DRM or licensing; static analysis first; rebuild as original designs and code; never copy assets, code, names, or IP.
 
 The art, UI, and sound variant in that skill tells an asset bot to distill how reference games store sprites, models, HUD layout, juice, and audio events, then generate original assets with local tools only. It forbids extracting the reference's actual art or audio. That is the recovered permission boundary for Lincoln's side of a reference pass. The REA server itself runs in a local Cursor client; these notes do not show Regliobecky or Lincoln calling it directly.
 
 A related cloud agent, `bc-750a35a2-7551-5ab7-a48c-2ab59ecdb581` ("testseed procgen library", Godot 4.8), does not mention either name. It shows the seed-engineering pattern beside them: modules with a seeded RNG (reproducible from an int) and pure functions, validators for connectivity, reachability, slope, and spacing, a demo scene with a seed field and a regenerate button, and headless tests that the same seed gives the same output. [INFERRED] Folding that pattern into these two agents is a synthesis. The pattern itself is recovered from that agent.
.
node evidence.dawn-whistle
parent: evidence
summary: Dawn Whistle recovered job: Scrapdune contact sheet, Unity 6, HUD prompt 15, Cinderstride gray-box. answers: batches and seeds
src: sources/evidence.md | terminals/800941.txt
see: evidence.regliobecky evidence.lincoln
requires: -
supersedes: -
derived-from: -
inferred: false
fold: expanded
body:
 
 Dawn Whistle is the job these notes actually describe. It is one instance of the protocol, not a rule that every future seed is a PS1 arena game.
 
 The spec file `dawn-whistle-spec.md` was read from terminal `800941.txt`:
 
 - Section 9, build order, starts at the PSX render pipeline and one rig (Cinderstride) in a gray-box Scrapdune Yard, and ends at step 7: polish, then split screen as a stretch goal. The middle steps are not in the snippet.
 - Section 10, asset list, marked "Lincoln, local tools only": low-poly models at 300–900 triangles (4 beasts, each with 3 detachable armor plate sets; 4 riders; 4 tools); arena kits (car bodies, a magnet crane, houses at 3 burn stages); textures at 64–128 px matching the seed screenshots; audio including radio barks by local text-to-speech only.
 
 The spec has 11 sections. Prompts 1–22 are separate build prompts. Prompt 15 is the HUD and must match the seed screenshots. The project named in the brief is Dawn Whistle, Unity 6 URP, "Use Unity MCP". The titles of the 11 sections other than the look sections, §9, and §10 were not recovered.
 
 The batch prompt pattern, shared by the four transcripts `bc-ce93cafa`, `bc-21289031`, `bc-cd4b2f50`, and `bc-c3ebd088`, is:
 
 > Batch name: X. Read uploads/common.md first (shared brief: tools, style, export, delivery). Build rows <IDs> from docs/asset-list.md. Build prompt N (docs/promptN.txt) is the scene that will use the kit, so give it every child pivot and socket it mentions. Seed NN is the look reference. The attached Scrapdune contact sheet is a finished kit from the same pipeline: match its style and quality bar; reuse scripts/scrapdune/. Don't rebuild shared props. Output to assets/... with a README and previews/ (contact sheet + 320×240 layout shot).
 
 Row ids that appear by name: TEX-04, TEX-08, VFX-04. P0 rows go first. [INFERRED] Wider prefixes such as CC, UI, and WPN, and the columns GENERATE / SOURCE / UNITY-SIDE, appear in the later draft prompt and not in these notes. P0 itself is recovered.
 
 The first accepted kit in this job was Scrapdune. Later batches were told to match its contact sheet. [INFERRED] The general rule "the newest finished kit becomes the bar" is that practice, stated as a rule.
.
